'''Banks Peninsula''' is a New Zealand parliamentary [[New Zealand electorates|electorate]] which initially existed from 1996 to 2008, and was later recreated in 2020 ahead of the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020 election]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://vote.nz/boundary-review-2019-2020/|title=Boundary Review 2019/20 &#124; Elections}}</ref> It was held by National's [[David Carter (politician)|David Carter]] for the initial term, and then by Labour's [[Ruth Dyson]] from 1999 to 2008.<ref>{{cite web |title=Hon Ruth Dyson |url=https://www.parliament.nz/en/mps-and-electorates/members-of-parliament/dyson-ruth/ |publisher=[[New Zealand Parliament]] |access-date=21 November 2019 |date=16 October 2019}}</ref> As of 2023, the seat is currently held by National's [[Vanessa Weenink]].

==Population centres==
The Banks Peninsula electorate, as defined in 2020, includes some of southern [[Christchurch]], including the suburbs of [[Oaklands,_New_Zealand|Oaklands]], [[Somerfield,_New_Zealand|Somerfield]], [[Cashmere,_New_Zealand|Cashmere]], [[Kennedys Bush]], [[Woolston, New Zealand|Woolston]], [[Halswell]], [[Heathcote Valley]], and [[Sumner,_New_Zealand|Sumner]]. It also includes some towns immediately south of Christchurch such as [[Lyttelton, New Zealand|Lyttelton]], [[Governors Bay]], [[Rāpaki]], [[Lansdowne,_Christchurch|Lansdowne]] and all of [[Banks Peninsula]] itself including the towns of [[Akaroa]], [[Diamond_Harbour,_New_Zealand|Diamond Harbour]], [[Duvauchelle]] and [[Little_River,_New_Zealand|Little River]].<ref name="parllib">{{cite web| url= https://www3.parliament.nz/en/mps-and-electorates/electorate-profiles/banks-peninsula-electorate-profile/ |title=Banks Peninsula - Electorate Profile |publisher= Parliamentary Library |date= 27 April 2021 |access-date= 21 September 2025}}</ref>

==History==
The [[1996 New Zealand general election|1996 election]] was notable for the significant change of electorate boundaries, based on the provisions of the Electoral Act 1993.<ref>{{cite act|type=Act|number=87|date=17 August 1993|title=Electoral Act 1993|url=http://www.legislation.co.nz/act/public/1993/0087/latest/whole.html#DLM307519|access-date=30 June 2015|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150710153411/http://www.legislation.co.nz/act/public/1993/0087/latest/whole.html|archive-date=10 July 2015}}</ref> Because of the introduction of the [[Mixed-member proportional representation|mixed-member proportional]] (MMP) electoral system, the number of electorates had to be reduced, leading to significant changes. More than half of the electorates contested in 1996 were newly constituted, and most of the remainder had seen significant boundary changes. In total, 73 electorates were abolished, 29 electorates were newly created (including Banks Peninsula), and 10 electorates were recreated, giving a net loss of 34 electorates. Banks Peninsula replaced the previous [[Lyttelton (New Zealand electorate)|Lyttelton electorate]].

In boundary changes for the [[2008 New Zealand general election|2008 general election]], the electorate lost its rural population centres to the newly formed [[Selwyn (New Zealand electorate)|Selwyn]], and became a solely urban electorate that was renamed [[Port Hills (New Zealand electorate)|Port Hills]]. Labour's Ruth Dyson retained Port Hills for all four elections with a greater lead than she had seen for Banks Peninsula. Dyson chose not to seek reelection in 2020 with Labour's [[Tracey McLellan]] succeeding Dyson.<ref name=":0">{{cite web|url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/118623020/the-battle-of-banks-peninsula-two-fresh-faces-contest-a-newly-competitive-seat |title= The Battle of Banks-Peninsula: two fresh faces contest a newly competitive seat |publisher=  Stuff (Fairfax) |date= 12 January 2020}}</ref>

{{maplink|from=New Zealand Electorates/2020/General/Banks Peninsula.map|frame=yes|text=Map of boundaries at the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020]] and [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023]] general elections}}
In the boundary review of 2019/2020, the Representation Commission decided to make large changes to the boundaries of Port Hills, taking area in [[Halswell]] and parts of [[Bromley, New Zealand|Bromley]] out and adding Banks Peninsula in, to manage large changes in population in the Christchurch and {{NZ electorate link|Selwyn}} areas. The electorate was also re-recreated as Banks Peninsula.<ref>{{cite web |title=Untitled (interactive map) |url=https://vote.nz/map/index.html?id=6&modified=20191115033549 |website=vote.nz |publisher=New Zealand Electoral Commission |access-date=21 November 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Law |first1=Tina |title=Boundary changes could swing Labour's safe Port Hills seat toward National |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/the-press/news/117562878/major-changes-for-christchurchs-selwyn-and-port-hills-electorates |access-date=21 November 2019 |work=[[The Press]] |date=20 November 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://vote.nz/assets/Boundary-Review/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2020.pdf|title=Report of the Representation Commission 2020|date=17 April 2020}}</ref> At its first election, the electorate was won easily by Labour's [[Tracey McLellan]] amidst the nationwide Labour landslide.

